From aa3cd392e64744e442dedb9215b0f17e4e0912ee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Felix <52862870+devilAPI@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 19:08:05 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 50/53] i2c: qcom-cci: no interrupt while runtime suspended

cci_isr() reads CCI_IRQ_STATUS_0 first thing. An interrupt that arrives
after the runtime suspend turned the CCI clocks off (a late halt/reset
acknowledgement after a transfer timed out or a client went away) makes
that read fault: on MSM8996 an asynchronous SError and a kernel panic
(LineageOS 24 on the ZTE Axon 7: panic right after an a0c000.cci
interrupt, ftrace dump in pstore, while a libcamera client was killed
mid-stream). Disable the interrupt before the clocks go off and enable it
again after they are back.

Ported from the LineageOS 24 kernel series (same mainline 7.3.1 base).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FVN6i49UMoL3MhGT7nwMzG
---
 drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-qcom-cci.c | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-qcom-cci.c b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-qcom-cci.c
index bd70c6bae..2c8af510d 100644
--- a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-qcom-cci.c
+++ b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-qcom-cci.c
@@ -475,6 +475,11 @@ static int __maybe_unused cci_suspend_runtime(struct device *dev)
 {
 	struct cci *cci = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
 
+	/*
+	 * No interrupt while the clocks are off: cci_isr() reads the status
+	 * register, and that read raises an SError on an unclocked CCI.
+	 */
+	disable_irq(cci->irq);
 	cci_disable_clocks(cci);
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -489,6 +494,7 @@ static int __maybe_unused cci_resume_runtime(struct device *dev)
 		return ret;
 
 	cci_init(cci);
+	enable_irq(cci->irq);
 	return 0;
 }
 
